'''Great Zhongyuan''' was the imperial state that dominated the [[Jade Peninsula]] after the collapse of [[Kingdom of Donghai|Donghai]] in 285 CE. Centered on the capital of [[Zhongjing]], it represented the most extensive political consolidation achieved in the peninsula during the long Zongic conflict cycle. Under [[King Yuanxiong of Zhongyuan|King Yuanxiong]], the state linked the coastal trade routes, inland grain lands, and southern marches into a single imperial system.

Great Zhongyuan remained the principal power of the peninsula until the [[War of the Four Sons]] shattered the royal succession. The resulting civil war ended the unity created after Donghai's fall and produced the successor kingdoms of [[Haizhou]], [[Nanyang]], [[Jiaozhi]], and [[Zhongyuan]]. Later historians treated Great Zhongyuan as both the high point of the ancient centralised state tradition and the immediate precursor to the later era of fragmentation.

== Geography ==

Great Zhongyuan occupied the northern lands of the Jade Peninsula, lying above the Huo River. Its territory included the central plain, the major inland river corridors, and the principal coastal basins north of the range. Beilong remained a separate frontier zone in the northwest, while the southern side of the Huo River was controlled by Chiang State. The mountain chain therefore functioned both as a defensive barrier and as a political boundary between the imperial core and the southern marches.

== Formation ==

Great Zhongyuan emerged after the final defeat of Donghai in 285 CE. The fall of [[Haicheng]] ended the long coastal rivalry that had dominated the peninsula for generations and removed the last major obstacle to Zhongyuan expansion. King Yuanxiong of Zhongyuan declared the new imperial state soon after the conquest, presenting it as the restoration of order across the Jade Peninsula rather than a simple territorial annexation.

The new state was built from earlier Zhongyuan institutions but expanded far beyond the old inland kingdom. Tributary systems were reorganised, port cities were brought under direct royal administration, and frontier districts were reclassified as imperial holdings. The state took its name from Zhongyuan because the central plain remained its political core, but its authority now reached from the coast to the far south.

== Government and administration ==

Great Zhongyuan was a highly centralised monarchy. Royal authority was concentrated at [[Zhongjing]], where the court supervised taxation, military appointments, river transport, and maritime customs. The king's administration attempted to balance the interests of the inland grain regions with the commercial needs of the coast, a task that had not been possible in the earlier era of divided kingdoms.

The court also invested heavily in infrastructure. Roads and river stations were repaired to move troops and grain more efficiently, while coastal fortifications were strengthened to secure trade routes and deter raiding. Great Zhongyuan was therefore not merely a conquest state; it was a governing system designed to hold a peninsula-wide polity together.

The state relied on a mixture of royal appointees, local magnates, and military governors. Some frontier regions were ruled indirectly through loyal elites, while strategically important ports and transport corridors were placed under tighter supervision. This structure allowed Great Zhongyuan to govern a large territory with relative stability during King Yuanxiong's lifetime.

== King Yuanxiong ==

King Yuanxiong of Zhongyuan was the founder and defining ruler of Great Zhongyuan. He had risen during the final stage of the long wars with Donghai and used the kingdom's collapse to proclaim a wider imperial order. Later tradition remembered him as a ruler of exceptional administrative talent, though also as one whose reign depended heavily on personal authority and dynastic prestige.

His reign established the imperial style of Great Zhongyuan. The court encouraged the idea of a single peninsula under one ruler, and Yuanxiong's government used the language of restoration, legitimacy, and unity to justify its rule. By the end of his reign, Great Zhongyuan had become the dominant state in the region and the principal political reference point for the peninsula.

== The War of the Four Sons ==
{{main|War of the Four Sons}}

The stability of Great Zhongyuan ended with Yuanxiong's death in 300 CE. His four sons each claimed a legitimate right to succeed him and each controlled a different regional power base. The resulting conflict became the [[War of the Four Sons]], which turned a succession dispute into a struggle for the entire imperial system.

{| class="wikitable"
! Claimant !! Base of power !! Later kingdom !! Regional support
|-
| Prince Hailin || Eastern coast || [[Haizhou]] || Port cities and maritime elites
|-
| Prince Nanyan || Southern interior || [[Nanyang]] || Grain regions and inland administrators
|-
| Prince Jiaozhe || Far south || [[Jiaozhi]] || Southern marches
|-
| Prince Zhongwei || Central capital || [[Zhongyuan]] || Court officials and central aristocracy
|}

At first, the war remained a contest among rival courts. Hailin held the coast, Nanyan dominated the inland roads, Jiaozhe controlled the southern frontier, and Zhongwei retained the imperial center. Over time, however, local commanders, merchants, and provincial elites began to align with whichever claimant could best protect their territory. The war therefore broke not only the royal family, but the political coherence of the empire itself.

By 318 CE, the struggle had exhausted the imperial army and destroyed the possibility of a single restoration. Great Zhongyuan continued to exist in name, but its authority had become fragmented and regional. The old imperial order survived only as a memory and a title.

== Fragmentation and successors ==

The breakup of Great Zhongyuan produced four successor kingdoms that reshaped the peninsula. [[Haizhou]] controlled the eastern coast and its ports. [[Nanyang]] inherited much of the southern interior and the grain-producing zones. [[Jiaozhi]] dominated the far south and the river corridors leading to the sea. [[Zhongyuan]] preserved the old capital and the symbolic core of the previous empire.

These states were not instantly stable. Rival claims, border disputes, and shifting alliances persisted for decades, and the peninsula remained divided well beyond the end of the civil war. By about 350 CE, however, the former imperial unity of Great Zhongyuan had fully dissolved, leaving a political landscape that later historians treated as the prelude to the next age of conflict and eventual reunification.

== Legacy ==

Great Zhongyuan occupied a central place in later Zongic historical memory. It demonstrated that the peninsula could be ruled as a single system, but it also showed how quickly such unity could collapse when succession failed. Later rulers of Zong would remember King Yuanxiong as both a model of imperial restoration and a warning about dynastic fragility.

The state also provided the institutional bridge between the old coastal world of Donghai and the later fragmented successor kingdoms. Its administrative reforms, road systems, and port controls became important precedents for later Zong polities. For this reason, Great Zhongyuan is usually treated not as an isolated empire, but as the decisive intermediate stage in the long history of Zongic state formation.

